      en: "The Royal Palace of Foumban is the historical and cultural centerpiece of the Bamum Kingdom in the West Region of Cameroon. The current building was completed in 1917 under the reign of Sultan Ibrahim Njoya, a visionary ruler who also invented the Bamum script. The palace's architecture is a unique blend of traditional African construction and European influences of the time, particularly German styles. The palace houses a significant museum that preserves an extensive collection of masks, weapons, statues, and royal regalia of the Bamum dynasty, dating back to the 14th century. Every year, the palace is the venue for the Nguon festival, where the rich history and cultural traditions of the people are celebrated. The structure is built from bricks and wood, which was a technical feat for the region at that time. As a symbol of continuity, the palace still serves as the official residence of the Sultan. It has been placed on the UNESCO World Heritage tentative list to highlight its exceptional importance to global cultural heritage.",
